<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altan Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1507–1582</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Altan Khan was leader of the Tümed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> who raided Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for decades. In 1571 he made peace with the Ming, gaining trade and a royal title, and he founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohhot">Hohhot</a>. In 1578 he met the Tibetan lama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonam-gyatso">Sonam Gyatso</a> and gave him the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, beginning the conversion of the Mongols to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug Buddhism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/altan-khan.jpg" alt="Altan Khan" width="150" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altan_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
